\section{Investigating Improvement Strategies}

We tested three diverse strategies to improve our frame‐level logistic regression baseline (cost 54.07): 

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Grid search on regularization} (Section~3.1)
  \item \textbf{Gaussian noise augmentation} (Section~3.2)
  \item \textbf{Per‐class threshold tuning} (Section~3.3)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{3.1 Hyperparameter Tuning (Grid Search on C)}
\label{sec:grid}
\paragraph{Idea \& Hypothesis.}
The inverse regularization strength $C$ controls the bias–variance trade‐off in logistic regression. We hypothesised that a smaller $C$ might reduce overfitting and thus lower the segment‐level cost.

\paragraph{Experiment.}
We trained one model per class for $C \in \{0.01,0.1,1,10,100\}$ and computed the total cost on our validation split:
\begin{table}
  \centering
  \caption{Validation cost vs.\ regularization parameter $C$.}
  \label{tab:grid}
  \begin{tabular}{cc}
    \toprule
    $C$ & Total cost per minute \\
    \midrule
    0.01 & 50.3383 \\
    0.10 & 51.0511 \\
    1.00 & 52.4959 \\
    10.00 & 52.7044 \\
    100.0 & 52.5129 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Outcome.}
The best cost occurred at $C=0.01$, lowering the total cost to 50.3383 (\textit{6.8\% drop}).

\subsection{3.2 Data Augmentation (Gaussian Noise)}
\label{sec:augment}
\paragraph{Idea \& Hypothesis.}
We injected Gaussian noise into each frame ($\sigma=0.02$, 3 copies) to diversify training data. We expected increased robustness and lower cost.

\paragraph{Experiment \& Results.}
We retrained the model at $C=0.01$ with noise augmentation and compared:
\begin{table}
  \centering
  \caption{Validation cost with and without Gaussian noise augmentation.}
  \label{tab:aug}
  \begin{tabular}{lcc}
    \toprule
    Model & Total cost \\
    \midrule
    LR ($C=0.01$) & 50.3383 \\
    LR + Noise Augmentation & 50.5702 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Outcome.}
Augmentation slightly increased cost to 50.5702, indicating simple frame‐wise noise did not simulate real acoustic variability effectively.

\subsection{3.3 Cost‐Specific Tuning (Per‐Class Thresholds)}
\label{sec:threshold}
\paragraph{Idea \& Hypothesis.}
A fixed probability threshold 0.5 may not be optimal for all classes. We searched thresholds in [0.1,0.9] (step 0.05) per class to minimise its segment‐level cost.

\paragraph{Experiment.}
For each class we chose the threshold that gave the lowest validation cost:

\begin{table}
  \centering
  \caption{Per‐class best thresholds and costs.}
  \label{tab:thr}
  \begin{tabular}{lcc}
    \toprule
    Class & Best Thr. & Cost \\
    \midrule
    Speech       & 0.50 & 6.1279  \\
    Shout        & 0.90 & 6.1577  \\
    Chainsaw     & 0.25 & 3.0320  \\
    Jackhammer   & 0.90 & 3.5172  \\
    Lawn Mower   & 0.65 & 4.1427  \\
    Power Drill  & 0.85 & 8.7387  \\
    Dog Bark     & 0.70 & 1.6682  \\
    Rooster Crow & 0.90 & 0.1851  \\
    Horn Honk    & 0.90 & 6.0066  \\
    Siren        & 0.90 & 5.2215  \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

Applying these thresholds yielded:

\begin{table}
  \centering
  \caption{Final validation performance with tuned thresholds.}
  \label{tab:final}
  \begin{tabular}{lcccc}
    \toprule
    Class & BAcc & Precision & Recall & F1 \\
    \midrule
    Speech       & 0.899 & 0.653 & 0.850 & 0.739 \\
    Shout        & 0.737 & 0.313 & 0.490 & 0.382 \\
    Chainsaw     & 0.802 & 0.673 & 0.607 & 0.638 \\
    Jackhammer   & 0.749 & 0.556 & 0.501 & 0.527 \\
    Lawn Mower   & 0.772 & 0.418 & 0.550 & 0.475 \\
    Power Drill  & 0.734 & 0.337 & 0.484 & 0.397 \\
    Dog Bark     & 0.897 & 0.634 & 0.806 & 0.710 \\
    Rooster Crow & 0.837 & 0.773 & 0.675 & 0.721 \\
    Horn Honk    & 0.753 & 0.304 & 0.518 & 0.383 \\
    Siren        & 0.836 & 0.748 & 0.676 & 0.710 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

The new total cost per minute is \textbf{44.7977}, a further \textit{11.1\% reduction} from the best grid‐search model.

